<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="using:System">

  <!--
    One family carries the whole product.

    Inter is bundled rather than hoped for: the package is referenced in the csproj and
    registered by Program.WithInterFont, so the name resolves to the shipped face on every
    platform and the app reads the same on all three. The chain behind it is the platform
    sans, which only ever runs if that registration is gone.

    The second family is gone. A sans/mono split says "this value came from a machine", but
    it says it by changing the shape of every letter mid-line, and the product's lines mix
    prose and figures constantly - a status bar segment, a chip's detail, a card's title.
    What the split was bought for is column alignment in a run of digits, and that is a font
    feature rather than a font: FigureFeatures below turns on Inter's tabular numerals, which
    gives every digit the same advance width without changing the face. Machine-ness is
    carried by colour and by position instead, which is what the rest of the design already
    does.
  -->

  <FontFamily x:Key="UiFont">Inter,Segoe UI,system-ui,sans-serif</FontFamily>

  <!--
    Tabular figures, for anything that is read as a column or that ticks: a timer whose
    digits must not shuffle the text beside them, a table cell, a plot's annotation. Prose
    keeps the proportional default, where a 1 next to a 4 is meant to look like prose.
  -->
  <FontFeatureCollection x:Key="FigureFeatures">+tnum</FontFeatureCollection>

  <!--
    The size ladder: four whole pixels, plus one promoted figure.

    It replaces a seven-step 11-to-16 set, and both changes are legibility rather than taste.
    The set was small - 11px badge glyphs and 13px body copy on a 1440p panel sit at or under
    the 12px floor every published desktop scale starts at, and the sentences this form is
    made of are the product's actual content rather than chrome. And seven steps inside a
    5px range is not a ladder anyone can hear: 13 beside 14 beside 15 reads as one size
    rendered unevenly, so the steps cost churn and bought no hierarchy.

    Four steps, each a clear jump, and one 26px figure at the top. Whole pixels only: a half
    step does not survive rasterisation, since 13.5px and 13px land on the same pixel grid at
    100% scaling.
  -->
  <!--
    Text that labels rather than says: the number inside a 16px step badge, a table's column
    keys, a chip's trailing count. The floor - nothing in the product is set smaller.
  -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeMinor">12</sys:Double>
  <!-- Detail beside the point of the line: a hint, a muted key, a figure, a column head. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeSmall">13</sys:Double>
  <!-- The default. A control's label, body copy, and the size a bare TextBlock renders at. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeBody">14</sys:Double>
  <!-- A panel's name, and the action that commits a screen. The largest text anywhere. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeHeading">16</sys:Double>
  <!-- The figures in the broadcast header, sized to read from across a room. -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizePromoted">26</sys:Double>

  <!--
    Line heights, for the two roles that wrap. A single-line label is left alone: setting a
    line box taller than the glyphs on a row that is already centred vertically only moves
    the text off the centre it was placed on.

    Both are ~1.4x their size, which is the ratio prose is comfortable at and the reason the
    numbers are stated rather than left to the face's own metrics - Inter's default leading
    is tighter than a paragraph of explanation wants.
  -->
  <sys:Double x:Key="LineHeightSmall">18</sys:Double>
  <sys:Double x:Key="LineHeightBody">20</sys:Double>

</ResourceDictionary>
